John Edward: Language skills essential for global citizens
Independent Schools of Scotland maintain a track record of academic excellence, continuing in 2018 with another set of excellent examination results, which is reinforced only by an individual and collective success in sport, arts, music and other community activities.
With more than 30,000 students across Scotland, these schools, represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), strive to give the best level of service to students and their parents.
Independent Schools are aimed at preparing their students for further education and higher education, their chosen career and their role as global citizens.
As an education sector that can design and implement a personalised school curriculum, we see that modern languages remain a subject of a popular and desirable choice in schools.
Nelson Mandela said: "If a person has spoken in a language that he understands, he understands.
If they spoke in his own language, he understands with his own heart."
This is a strong reminder that we cannot rely solely on English when we need to build relationships and trust with people from other countries.
From the results of this year's recent extinuation, it can be seen that the languages are at the forefront of the  ranking tables with the highest rates of success in independent schools.
A total of 68 per cent of students who have studied foreign-language have won A higher grade.
The data, collected from the 74 schools who are members of the SCIS, showed that 72 per cent of the students gained Higher grade in Mandarin, while 72 per cent of those studying in German, 69 per cent of French and 63 per cent Spanish studyees also gained A.
This illustrates that independent schools in Scotland are supporting foreign languages as a vital skills that children and young people will no doubt be needed in the future.
Languages now, as a subject's choice, are being maintained in the same way as STEM (science, technology, engineering and mathematics) in the curricula of independent schools and elsewhere.
A survey by the United Kingdom's Commission for Employment and Skills in 2014 found that out of the reasons that employers have been able to fill job vacancies, 17 percent were attributed to a lack of language skills.
Therefore, language skills are becoming more and more perpetrators to prepare young people for their future careers.
With more prospective job opportunities requiring languages, these skills are essential in a globalised world.
Irrespective of the career to choose whether to learn another language, it will have a real advantage in the future as it is able to survive such a lifetime.
Being able to communicate directly with people from foreign countries will automatically put a multilingual person at the forefront of the competition.
According to an YouGov survey of more than 4,000 United Kingdom adults in 2013, 75 percent were not able to speak a foreign language well enough to maintain a conversation and, with 15 per cent, the French is the only language spoken with a percentage of two digits.
This is why investing in language learning is now important for today's children.
Knowing more than one language, particularly those of developing economies, children will have a better chance to find a significant job.
In Scotland, each school will learn different languages.
A number of schools will focus on the more classic modern languages, while others will teach languages that are considered to be the most important for the UK when looking forward to 2020, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
Whatever your child's interest, there will always be a number of languages to be able to choose from them in independent schools, with teachers who are specialist in this field.
Scottish independent Schools are dedicated to providing a learning environment that will prepare children and give them the skills needed to succeed, regardless of the future.
It cannot currently be denied, in a global business environment that languages remain very important for the country's future, so this should be reflected in education.
Indeed, modern languages should truly be seen as "international communication skills."
Independent Schools will continue to offer this choice, diversity and excellence to young people of Scotland.
The faut bien le faire.
John Edward is Director of the Independent School Scottish Council
