The course, which started for the first time this year, explains to students the essential things about how good sleep traditions help academic and Athletic performance, as well as improve their overall well-being.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at Harvard's Medical School and an executive director of university's health services, said that the university decided to introduce the course after she found that the students had a serious lack of sleep during the week.
The hour-life course involves a series of interactive tasks.
In one section, there is an image of a surfroom where students click on coffee cups, curtainers, trainers and books to tell them the effects of caffeine and lighting and on how haletic performance has an effect on sleep deficiency, and the routine importance of sleeping time.
