How the US-China trade war can be good for Beijing
The numbers of bombs of the opening of the US-China commerce war surrected, and while the battle has continued to end, a lack of agreement between countries could be beneficial for Beijing in the long-term, the experts.
Donald Trump, the President of the United States, gave the first earlier warning this year by taxing the leading Chinese exporters including solar, iron and aluminium panels.
The most significant increase has reached this week with new tariffs affecting goods amounting to EUR 200 billion (EUR 150 billion), effectively tax all goods entering the USA from China.
Beijing irritated every time in the same way, while it was more recently presenting fees of up to ten per cent on EUR 60 billion of American goods.
China has pledged to agree with the United States every time, and the world's second largest economy is unlikely to succeed in the near future.
Washington puts a step back means accepting the requests, but publicly the fact that China surrenders to the United States will be too embarrassing for Xi Jinping, the president of China.
However, the experts say that if Beijing works wisely, the pressures of the U.S. trade war could support China in a long-term positive way by reducing the interdependency of the two economies.
"The fact that a political decision in Washington or Beijing can create the conditions for starting economic chaos in both countries is in fact much more dangerous than what they have recognised the spectators earlier, said Abigail Grace, a research partner that focuses on Asia at the Center for New American Security (Center for New American Security).
Syria stands ready for refugees to return, says Minister for Foreign Affairs
Syria states that stands ready for the voluntary return of the refugees and is calling for help to rebuild the country destroyed by war that lasted for more than seven years.
Speaking to the unitary Nations General Assembly Walid al-Moourem, the Foreign Minister Walid al-Moourem said the conditions in the country are improving.
Today the situation on the ground is more stable and secure thanks to the progress made in the fight against terrorism.
The government continues to rehabilitate the areas destroyed by terrorists to restore normality.
All the conditions are now present for the voluntary return of refugees in the country that they had to leave because of terrorism and the unilateral economic measures targeting their daily lives and their livelihoods.
The UN estimates that since the beginning of the war in 2011 more than 5.5 million Syrians have left the country.
Another six million people who still live in the country need humanitarian assistance.
Al-Moualem said that the Syrian regime would welcome help to rebuild the destroyed country.
But he empha insisted that it would not accept conditional assistance or assistance from countries sponsored sedition.
