Adolence on Red Tide: Concentrations decline in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota
The most recent report from Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission shows an overall decrease in Red Tide concentrations for parts of the Tampa Bay area.
According to the FWC, conditions of overgrowth of algae in isolate areas are being reported in Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier - suggesting a decrease in concentrations.
Red Tide which is an excessive growth of algae it extends around 130 miles of the coast from the top of Pinellas to the countries below Lee.
Isolated areas can be found around 10 miles off the coast of Hillsborough County, but at least last week-related sites.
Red Tide has also been observed in Pasco County.
Mean concentrations close to the coast or off the coast of Pinellas County have been reported in the last week, low-to-high concentrations off the coast of Hillsborough County, for high concentrations at the Manatee County, a background for near-coastal high concentrations or off the coast of Sarasota County, a background for average concentrations at Charlotte Valuity, a background for near-coastal or offshore Lee County, and low concentrations in the Collier County.
Respiratory irritation will continue to be reported in the counties of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier.
